---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { articleSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../lib/schema';
import CircleMiniPreview from '../components/CircleMiniPreview.astro';
import { generateCircle } from '../core/geometry';
import { rowInstruction } from '../lib/buildPlan';
const examples=[11,21,31].map(size=>({size,result:generateCircle(size)}));
const title='How to Make a Circle in Minecraft – Step-by-Step Block Guide | VoxelCurve';
const description='Learn how to make a circle in Minecraft using center points, anchors, row segments and symmetry. Includes practical 11×11, 21×21 and 31×31 building examples.';
const canonical='https://voxelcurve.com/how-to-make-a-circle-in-minecraft';
const schemas=[
  breadcrumbSchema([{name:'VoxelCurve',url:'https://voxelcurve.com/'},{name:'How to Make a Circle in Minecraft',url:canonical}]),
  articleSchema({headline:'How to Make a Circle in Minecraft',url:canonical,description})
];
---
<BaseLayout {title} {description} {canonical} type="article" {schemas}>
<section class="resource-hero"><div class="container"><div class="resource-hero-grid"><div><span class="eyebrow">Builder guide</span><h1>How to Make a Circle in Minecraft</h1><p>A practical construction method for small and large block circles—whether you searched how to make, create or build a circle in Minecraft—without trying to eyeball the curve.</p></div><div class="resource-meta"><span>ROW-BY-ROW GUIDE</span><strong>Center → Segments</strong><small>Practical construction workflow</small></div></div></div></section>
<article class="section guide-article"><div class="container editorial-grid">
  <aside class="editorial-aside"><div class="section-kicker">Circle build method</div><p>Choose the footprint, establish symmetry, then follow the exact row segments.</p><a class="aside-cta" href="/#c-31-thin-1">Open 31×31 blueprint →</a></aside>
  <div class="prose editorial-prose">
    <h2>What makes a Minecraft circle?</h2>
    <p>A Minecraft circle approximates a curve using square blocks. Its outline consists of horizontal runs that get shorter or move inward as the curve turns. Matching the runs across the center creates a balanced shape; there is no single diagonal block that replaces the whole curve.</p>
    <h2>Odd vs. even sizes</h2>
    <p>An odd diameter, such as 11, 21 or 31, has one center block. An even diameter, such as 20, has a center gap between four blocks. Neither is better: choose the parity that fits your entrance, path or surrounding build. Keep that center convention throughout construction.</p>
    <h2>Choose the diameter</h2>
    <p>The diameter is the full outside width, including both edge blocks. A 21×21 circle fits inside a square 21 blocks wide and 21 blocks deep. Choose <strong>Thin</strong> for an outline, <strong>Thick</strong> for an inward-growing wall or <strong>Filled</strong> for a floor. Increasing thickness does not enlarge the footprint.</p>
    <h2>Find the center</h2>
    <p>For an 11×11 circle, mark the sixth block in each direction as the center. For 21×21, use the eleventh; for 31×31, use the sixteenth. This leaves 5, 10 or 15 blocks on each side. For a 20×20 footprint, mark the gap between columns 10 and 11 and between rows 10 and 11.</p>
    <h2>Mark four anchors</h2>
    <p>Mark the north, south, east and west extremes of the footprint. For a 21×21 odd circle, these are 10 blocks from the center, so the center plus both sides totals 21. For an even circle, use the blueprint's pair of center rows and columns rather than inventing one center block.</p>
    <h2>Understand rows and segments</h2>
    <p>Rows count from the top edge of the blueprint. A segment is one uninterrupted horizontal run. Place its first block at the stated center offset, then continue to the right for the stated length, including that first block. A gap is the number of empty columns between the end of one segment and the start of the next.</p>
    <div class="callout"><strong>Example:</strong> “Start 6 blocks left of center · place 3 right” means start at that offset and place three blocks in total. It does not mean place a starting block plus three additional blocks.</div>
    <h2>Build one quarter</h2>
    <p>Start at an anchor and follow the runs toward the neighboring anchor. When a segment crosses the center line, use only the part that belongs to your quarter. Keep the center and anchor markers separate from the outline materials so temporary markers are easy to remove.</p>
    <h2>Mirror the pattern</h2>
    <p>Reflect the completed quarter horizontally and vertically around the marked center. Center blocks and center-line segments belong to both halves, so place them once. Alternatively, follow complete rows with VoxelCurve's guided navigator and let the paired segments establish the symmetry.</p>
    <h2>Real examples: 11×11, 21×21 and 31×31</h2>
    <div class="guide-example-grid">
      {examples.map(({size,result})=><div>
        <a class="guide-example" href={`/#c-${size}-thin-1`}><CircleMiniPreview size={size} /><div><h3>{size}×{size}</h3><p>{result.blockCount} outline blocks · {Math.floor(result.blockCount/64)} stacks + {result.blockCount%64} loose</p><span>Open blueprint →</span></div></a>
        <div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>First three rows of the {size}×{size} thin outline</caption><thead><tr><th>Row</th><th>Place these segments</th><th>Empty gap</th></tr></thead><tbody>{result.rows.slice(0,3).map(row=><tr><td>{row.y+1}</td><td>{rowInstruction(row,size).map(instruction=><div>{instruction.center} (columns {instruction.span.start+1}–{instruction.span.end+1})</div>)}</td><td>{row.spans.length>1?row.spans[1].start-row.spans[0].end-1:'—'}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
      </div>)}
    </div>
    <h2>How to make a large circle in Minecraft</h2>
    <p>For a 31×31 or larger footprint, mark the center axes with temporary blocks and keep the tool open in Build Mode. Work by row or segment, mark progress after placement and use Undo immediately after a mistaken completion. Fit restores the full blueprint after panning or zooming. Material totals exclude temporary markers.</p>
    <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
    <ul><li>Using radius as diameter: measure the entire outside width.</li><li>Moving an even circle onto one center block: keep the four-block center gap.</li><li>Adding a starting block to the stated segment length: the first block is already included.</li><li>Counting the gap from a segment's start: count empty columns after its last block.</li><li>Duplicating blocks on a mirrored center line: overlapping center positions are placed once.</li><li>Sharing a link to transfer progress: links share the blueprint, while progress stays on the current device.</li></ul>
    <h2>Which circle-building question does this guide answer?</h2>
    <p>This circle Minecraft guide follows one method for the questions “how to make a Minecraft circle”, “how to make circle in Minecraft”, “how to build a circle in Minecraft” and “how to create a circle in Minecraft”. The shorter search “Minecraft how to make a circle” leads to the same practical steps: choose a diameter, mark the center, place the anchors and mirror the row segments. Use the examples above to check each step before scaling up.</p>
    <p><a class="text-link" href="/#c-31-thin-1">Open the 31×31 Minecraft Circle Generator →</a> to follow exact segments, track remaining materials and save a PNG, TXT or Litematic plan. The <a class="text-link" href="/minecraft-circle-chart">Circle Chart</a> compares common footprints before you build.</p>
  </div>
</div></article>
<section class="section related-section"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-kicker">Related tools</div>
  <h2>Use the method with a live blueprint</h2>
  <div class="related-list">
    <a href="/"><span class="related-index">01</span><div><h3>Circle Generator</h3><p>Generate custom diameters with exact row and segment instructions.</p></div><span class="related-arrow">↗</span></a>
    <a href="/minecraft-circle-chart"><span class="related-index">02</span><div><h3>Circle Chart</h3><p>Compare common circle sizes and block counts before choosing a footprint.</p></div><span class="related-arrow">↗</span></a>
    <a href="/minecraft-dome-generator"><span class="related-index">03</span><div><h3>Dome Generator</h3><p>Extend circular geometry upward into a guided layered roof.</p></div><span class="related-arrow">↗</span></a>
  </div>
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
